WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.520
When movies as popular as the Back&nbsp;
to the Future trilogy come along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.520
fans always end up wanting more.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.680
"You know that new sound you've been&nbsp;
looking for? Well, listen to this!"

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.920
And while it's been more than 30 years&nbsp;
since the release of the 1990 smash hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.080
Back to the Future Part III, beloved actor&nbsp;
Christopher Lloyd seems game to reprise his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.240
iconic role of eccentric inventor&nbsp;
Doc Brown. In a 2015 interview to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.160
celebrate the 30th anniversary of the first&nbsp;
film, Lloyd told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.480
"I would love to do Doc again, no question."

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.960
But wanting to do another movie is much easier&nbsp;
than actually making one. Of course, there's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.120
tremendous amount of time and resources that&nbsp;
go into any big budget Hollywood production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.520
But there's also the very real risk that things&nbsp;
might not turn out as well as the studio — or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.160
the fans — might hope. Beyond all that, however,&nbsp;
there's one more major factor that will dictate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.320
whether Lloyd would reprise his role for&nbsp;
a theoretical Back to the Future Part IV.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.920
The Back to the Future trilogy tackles&nbsp;
many themes, not the least of which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.040
the relationship between Marty and&nbsp;
Doc Brown. It's a friendship that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.960
stronger than ever in real life too, and&nbsp;
without Michael J. Fox's participation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.034
Lloyd told The Hollywood Reporter that he&nbsp;
would put the kibosh on his return for Part IV.

00:01:05.034 --> 00:01:07.040
"Great Scott!"

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.640
In fact, Lloyd said he would love to see all&nbsp;
the creatives behind the trilogy back for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.680
another Future trip. Referring to Fox's&nbsp;
1991 Parkinson's diagnosis, Lloyd said:

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.000
"I think regardless of the Parkinson's, he'd&nbsp;
probably jump on the role, and he'd be great.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.600
I can't see doing another sequel without&nbsp;
him. I would certainly want Bob Zemeckis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.280
directing it, Bob Gale writing it and&nbsp;
the other principals being involved."

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.200
Of course, maybe it's no surprise that he'd&nbsp;
want Fox to join him on the next adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.120
considering how much they enjoy working&nbsp;
together. In an exclusive interview with Looper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.360
Lloyd looked back at his relationship with Fox.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:50.480
"It's a thrill whenever we get together. He has&nbsp;
just such a loving, wonderful sense of himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:57.200
in his situation in time. Really, he just&nbsp;
has a perspective, and a sense of humor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:04.800
about it, and a kind of joy.&nbsp;
He loves being who he is."

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.200
Lloyd also told The Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter that they'd have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.560
bring back the trilogy's iconic&nbsp;
DeLorean, of which Lloyd said:

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.320
"It was such a perfect choice for the&nbsp;
film. It looked so speedy and futuristic."

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.160
One thing Lloyd doesn't&nbsp;
want to see happen, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.880
is a full-blown reboot of the franchise. He said:

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.640
"It's hard to replace Marty. Michael&nbsp;
J. Fox was so wonderful in the role."

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.400
Sadly, even if the stars were to align&nbsp;
for another Back to the Future project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.480
other variables would have to fall into place&nbsp;
too. Most importantly, according to Lloyd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.320
the fourth movie's story would have to rival&nbsp;
that of the first three. One thing that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.720
certainly be said for the original trilogy is&nbsp;
that each movie had a genuinely original plot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.920
Back to the Future saw Marty forced to keep&nbsp;
the past on track after traveling back to 1955;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.920
Part II found Marty and Doc having to&nbsp;
avert a terrible future; and Part III had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.040
them both travel to the year 1885, in a total&nbsp;
shake-up of the franchise's sci-fi stylings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.440
They're great stories — and Lloyd is well&nbsp;
aware of this. He told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.160
"It's tough to come up with an idea&nbsp;
that contains the excitement of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.880
original three. [...] But it&nbsp;
could be done, you never know."

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.120
But while a new movie might not be on the cards&nbsp;
just yet, Back to the Future fans can at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.320
enjoy a trip down memory lane. In the spring&nbsp;
of 2021, Lloyd joined host Josh Gates in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.480
Discovery+ series Expedition: Back to the&nbsp;
Future. Over the course of four episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.320
Gates and Lloyd hit the road to locate the&nbsp;
original movie's A-car — the DeLorean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.320
was backed out of Doc Brown's moving truck at&nbsp;
the mall in the first film. The goal, after they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.720
locate it, is to donate the car to the Michael&nbsp;
J. Fox Foundation for Parkinson's Research.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.640
In his interview with Looper, Lloyd said that&nbsp;
he essentially reprised his role as Doc Brown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.720
in the series, because, in real life, he and&nbsp;
the character are essentially one in the same.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:43.920
"So, I wasn't doing a character, I was&nbsp;
being myself. Usually when I do [a role],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:52.000
it involves taking on another life.&nbsp;
The one I'm taking on in this instance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:58.800
is my own, and so it had some intricate&nbsp;
areas, in my head anyway, to explore."

